<article class="env-lemma" id="0EPE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EPE">Lemma <span data-tag="0EPE">82.11.2</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0EDT" data-tag="0EDT">82.2.1</a> let $X, Y/B$ be good. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a finite locally free morphism of degree $d$ (see Morphisms of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/03ZV" data-tag="03ZV">67.46.2</a>). Then $f$ is both proper and flat of relative dimension $0$, and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  f_*f^*\alpha = d\alpha  \]
</div>
  <p> for every $\alpha \in Z_ k(Y)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      A finite locally free morphism is flat and finite by Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0416" data-tag="0416">67.46.6</a>, and a finite morphism is proper by Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/04NZ" data-tag="04NZ">67.45.9</a>. We omit showing that a finite morphism has relative dimension $0$. Thus the formula makes sense. To prove it, let $Z \subset Y$ be an integral closed subscheme of $\delta $-dimension $k$. It suffices to prove the formula for $\alpha = [Z]$. Since the base change of a finite locally free morphism is finite locally free (Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03ZY" data-tag="03ZY">67.46.5</a>) we see that $f_*f^*\mathcal{O}_ Z$ is a finite locally free sheaf of rank $d$ on $Z$. Thus clearly $f_*f^*\mathcal{O}_ Z$ has length $d$ at the generic point of $Z$. Hence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  f_*f^*[Z] = f_*f^*[\mathcal{O}_ Z]_ k = [f_*f^*\mathcal{O}_ Z]_ k = d[Z]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where we have used Lemmas <a href="/tag/0EPB" data-tag="0EPB">82.10.5</a> and <a href="/tag/0EP3" data-tag="0EP3">82.8.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>